[Scriptoscope](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope), my project that renders classic Mac OS Kaleidoscope themes in the browser, can now make new schemes as well as read old ones. This week it got an authoring toolkit and its first original scheme, Halcyon: light, frosted and modern, and deliberately not a copy of macOS. It loads in the real Kaleidoscope 2.3.1 on System 7.5.5, which is the screenshot above. The work is in [draft PR #540](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope/pull/540).

Every control in Halcyon was drawn by code I wrote by hand. That left me with a question: can AI tools draw this kind of art, from the command line, without me signing up for another paid API key?

Mostly yes, but not the way I expected.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - An image generator asked for a 16×16 PNG returned a 1,024×1,024 JPEG with a fake transparency checkerboard painted in. Cleanup tools could rescue a picture from it, but never the size I asked for
> - Asking a language model for text instead worked first time: rows of characters, or drawing code, at the exact size and palette
> - The best zero-key result was [claude-pxl](https://github.com/crsolver/claude-pxl), a "pixel art as code" Agent Skill. With its docs as context, Gemini wrote all eight Halcyon control sprites in one go, and they went through the real pipeline untouched
> - Paid pixel-art services exist and look promising; I have not tried them yet

## Why this art is awkward

A Kaleidoscope scheme is a binary resource fork full of tiny icons (the `cicn` resource), each with a 1-bit mask, at most 256 colours and an exact pixel size. A checkbox is a checkbox at 14×14 or 16×16 and nothing else. Some icons also carry hidden "swatch" pixels that the engine samples for colours. There is no anti-aliasing to hide behind, and no partial transparency: a pixel is in the mask or it is not.

That is a long way from what image models are built for. But it is also a very small amount of information. A 16×16 icon is 256 decisions, which made me wonder whether a model would do better writing the decisions down than painting them.

## Route A: ask an image generator

I started with the [Google Antigravity CLI](https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/reference) (`agy` 1.4.10), because I already had it and it has an image generator subagent. I asked for a 16×16 PNG checkbox, saved to a path in the repo.

