As a picture, it is nice. As an asset, almost nothing about it was what I asked for:

- It was a 1,024×1,024 JPEG, with about 5,000 colours of compression noise
- The "transparent" background is a checkerboard painted into the image
- It was drawn on a grid of about 36 cells, with the box 26 cells wide, not 16
- It was saved to the tool's own artifact folder (`~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/<conversation-id>/`), not the path I gave it

None of that is the model misbehaving. The `generate_image` tool only takes a prompt, a name, an aspect ratio and reference images. There is no parameter for size, format, transparency or output path, and image generation is not in the official CLI docs at all. The model was aiming for a picture *of* pixel art, and that is what it made.

### Cleaning it up

Two open-source tools exist for exactly this job: take a fake pixel-art image and recover the real grid.

- [unfake](https://github.com/painebenjamin/unfake.py) detected the grid correctly (a pitch of 28 pixels), then exited without writing any output and without an error
- [proper-pixel-art](https://github.com/KennethJAllen/proper-pixel-art) recovered a true 36×36 grid with clean alpha. It also faithfully kept the painted checkerboard, now as a real alternating pattern of pixels, and merged the two teals, so the border disappeared

