Text works because the model has to commit to every pixel. There is nothing to approximate, and the result is something I can read, diff and edit by hand.

### Which model?

`agy` lets you pick between several models, so I ran route B on seven of them, with the same prompt and an automated checker for size, palette and a few shape rules.

<figure class="kh-figure">
  <img class="kh-figure__image" src="/images/blog/pixel-art-model-comparison-rows.png" width="1524" eleventy:formats="png" alt="Eight enlarged teal checkboxes in a row, each labelled with a model name and a score. The first six, from Gemini 3.8 Flash, Gemini 4 Argon and Claude Sonnet and Opus 5.5, all have white check marks and pass 5 of 5 checks. The last two, both from GPT-OSS-120B, pass 4 of 5: one has a broken diagonal line instead of a check, and the other is a small square with teal blocks in its corners.">
  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">Seven models on the same 14×14 checkbox prompt. GPT-OSS-120B is on the right, twice.</figcaption>
</figure>

- Six of the seven passed every automated rule
- Claude Opus 5.5 Medium was the best overall and also fast: about 33 seconds, against about 200 seconds for most of the Gemini runs (I ran them concurrently, so some of that is contention)
- Gemini 3.8 Flash High tied with it on looks, but was slow
- GPT-OSS-120B failed: one run got a 503 "no capacity", and the retry drew nonsense

To be honest about the result: at 14 pixels, all of the checkboxes read slightly octagonal, and the automated rules did not separate the good ones from the merely passing ones. I still had to look.

I also ran route C on two models for the title bar buttons (close, zoom and collapse). Opus drew them cleanly. Gemini's plus sign sits off-centre, and both models' × marks have a heavy centre at 12 pixels.

