## Gotchas running agy headless

If you try this with `agy -p` (print mode) in a script, a few things caught me out:

- Tools are auto-denied in print mode. That is fine for text-only routes, but it did try to run a shell command after I told it not to
- Despite `--json-schema`, the JSON sometimes arrived twice, or wrapped in a `` ```json `` fence. Parse defensively
- Print mode exits before subagents finish unless you tell it to wait
- `--mode auto` is not available on some accounts

## What I did not try

Some services are built for this, but they need keys or subscriptions, so for now this is reading, not testing:

- **Retro Diffusion** is the only image model I found that takes exact small sizes (12 to 512 pixels), with `remove_bg` and a palette lock. Pricing is roughly $0.03 per image for fast, $0.06 for plus and $0.18 for pro, and their pixel fixer is $0.002. Their [API examples](https://github.com/Retro-Diffusion/api-examples) are a good place to start
- **PixelLab** has a pixel-art model on subscription. Most of its tools start at 32 pixels, which is bigger than most Kaleidoscope controls, and I have not verified its prices
- **OpenRouter** has about 20 general image models, at roughly 1 to 13 cents an image according to [OpenRouter's own September 2026 comparison](https://openrouter.ai/blog/insights/image-generation-models-compared/). Outputs start at 1,024 pixels and I could not find transparency documented, so I would expect the same grid problem as route A
- **Hugging Face Inference Providers** pass provider costs through, and a PRO account includes $2 a month of credits ([pricing](https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-providers/pricing)). There are community pixel-art LoRAs, but I did not find a priced hosted endpoint for one

There are also Agent Skills besides claude-pxl: a Retro Diffusion skill in [PlayableIntelligence/game-creator](https://github.com/PlayableIntelligence/game-creator), and various skills for Google's Nano Banana, which is general image generation and so has the same grid problem.

Things still on my list: trials of Retro Diffusion and PixelLab, feeding the checker's errors back to the model in a loop, generating a whole window frame rather than single controls, and running more than two models on route C.

## What I am taking from this

For exact-format pixel art, ask the model for code or text grids, not pictures. An image model aims for a picture of pixel art; text makes it decide each pixel, and what comes back stays editable. Image models are still useful for mood boards. If you have to use one for the asset itself, prompt for a flat key-colour background and run a grid-recovery tool like proper-pixel-art afterwards, then expect to fix the size yourself.

If you have tried Retro Diffusion or PixelLab on very small sprites, or you have a Kaleidoscope scheme you would like to see in a browser, I would like to hear about it. The Halcyon work and the authoring toolkit are in [PR #540](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope/pull/540).

